Output as JSON for scripting:

[107m [0m [2m[0m[2m[34mwt[0m[2m list [0m[2m[36m--format=json[0m

[1m[32mColumns[0m

  Column                                  Shows                                 
 ──────── ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 Branch   Branch name; a detached worktree has none, so it shows its short hash 
          in dim yellow                                                         
 Status   Compact symbols (see below)                                           
 HEAD±    Uncommitted changes, including untracked files: +added -deleted lines 
 main↕    Commits ahead/behind default branch                                   
 main…±   Line diffs since the merge-base (three-dot) with the default branch   
 Summary  LLM-generated branch summary; requires [2m--full[0m, [2msummary = true[0m, and    
          [2mcommit.generation[0m                                                     
 Remote⇅  Commits ahead/behind tracking branch                                  
 CI       PR/MR number colored by pipeline status; [2m--full[0m only                  
 Path     Worktree directory                                                    
 URL      Dev server URL from project config; dimmed if port is not listening   
 (custom) User-defined custom columns from [2m[list.custom-columns][0m user config    
          [experimental]                                                        
 Commit   Short hash, abbreviated per [2mcore.abbrev[0m                               
 Age      Time since last commit                                                
 Message  Last commit message (truncated)                                       

The [2mmain[0m header label is used regardless of the default branch's actual name.

The table sizes itself to the terminal. When the columns don't all fit, the 
least important go first — roughly right to left, since the order above runs 
from identity to nice-to-have — and the summary footer names them ([2mhidden: 
[2mCommit, Age, Message[0m). A wider terminal brings them back. To pin a set rather 
than leave it to the width, name the columns in [2m[list] columns[0m; [2m--format=json[0m 
carries every field at any width.

[2mmain↕[0m and [2mmain…±[0m measure against the default branch's upstream tip when the 
local copy lags it — so in a fork whose local [2mmain[0m trails [2morigin/main[0m, a branch 
reads as ahead of the real mainline, not of a stale local checkout. The [2m↑[0m/[2m↓[0m/[2m↕[0m 
Status symbols derive from these counts, so they track the upstream tip too.

[32mGutter[0m

The leftmost column marks each row by physical presence, from most present to 
least:

 Symbol                           Meaning                            
 ────── ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 [2m@[0m      Current worktree                                             
 [2m^[0m      Primary worktree (the repo's home worktree)                  
 [36m+[0m      Other worktree                                               
 [2m/[0m      Local branch without a worktree ([2m--branches[0m)                 
 [2m|[0m      Remote branch, not present locally until fetched ([2m--remotes[0m) 

[32mCI status[0m

The CI column shows the branch's open PR/MR — [2m#3035[0m on GitHub, Gitea, and Azure 
DevOps, [2m!3035[0m on GitLab — colored by pipeline status, or a bare [2m#[0m when no number
 is available (e.g. branch workflows without a PR/MR). One color folds two JSON 
fields: green/blue/red/yellow/gray are the pipeline state, magenta/cyan the 
review state. The [2mValue[0m column names each state; checks object and review states
 give the JSON spelling — schema 2 reports three of the pipeline values by the 
shape of [2mpr[0m and [2mchecks[0m rather than as a [2mchecks.status[0m string:

 Indicator        Value                             Meaning                     
 ───────── ──────────────────── ─────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 [32m#[0m green   [2m"passed"[0m             All checks passed                               
 [34m#[0m blue    [2m"running"[0m            Checks in progress                              
 [31m#[0m red     [2m"failed"[0m             One or more checks failed                       
 [33m#[0m yellow  [2m"conflicts"[0m          Merge conflicts with the target branch          
 [90m#[0m gray    [2m"no-ci"[0m              No PR/MR, or no checks configured               
 [33m⚠[0m yellow  [2m"error"[0m              CI status could not be fetched (rate limit,     
                                network, etc.)                                  
 [35m#[0m magenta [2m"changes_requested"[0m  A reviewer requested changes                    
 [36m#[0m cyan    [2m"pending"[0m            A review is required (e.g. branch protection)   
                                but not yet given                               
 (blank)   [2mpr[0m and [2mchecks[0m absent No upstream, or no PR/MR and no branch workflow 

The two remaining review states have no indicator of their own: [2m"draft"[0m only 
dims the cell and [2m"approved"[0m leaves the color unchanged.

Color precedence resolves the fold: changes-requested (magenta) outranks running
 checks — waiting can't clear it — while an outstanding required review (cyan) 
only recolors an otherwise green or quiet branch. Cool colors mean waiting, warm
 colors mean act. An approved PR, or one with no review signal at all (no 
required reviewers and no reviews), keeps its plain pipeline color — [2mpr.review[0m 
is then [2m"approved"[0m or absent, respectively. GitLab MR data carries only 
[2m"pending"[0m and [2m"draft"[0m — no approved or changes-requested signal.

CI cells are clickable links to the PR or pipeline page, and appear dimmed for a
 draft PR/MR ([2m"draft"[0m) or when unpushed local changes make the status stale 
([2mchecks.stale[0m). PRs/MRs are checked first, then branch workflows/pipelines for 
branches with an upstream. Local-only branches show blank; remote-only branches 
— visible with [2m--remotes[0m — get CI status detection. Results are cached for 30-60
 seconds; use [2mwt config state[0m to view or clear.

[32mLLM summaries[0m

Reuses the [2mcommit.generation[0m command — the same LLM that generates commit 
messages. Enable with [2msummary = true[0m in [2m[list][0m config; requires [2m--full[0m. Results 
are cached until the branch's diff changes.

[32mCustom columns [experimental][0m

Each [2m[list.custom-columns][0m entry in user config adds a column: the key is the 
header, the template renders each row's cell. Templates read two per-branch 
namespaces — [2m{{ vars.* }}[0m, stored with [2mwt config state vars set[0m, and [2m{{ 
[2mgit.branch.* }}[0m, the branch's own git config under [2mbranch.<name>.*[0m (a [2mjira[0m key 
you set yourself, or the git-native [2mdescription[0m) — useful for tracking what each
 of many (often agent-driven) branches is for:

[107m [0m [2m[36m[list.custom-columns.Ticket][0m
[107m [0m [2mtemplate = [0m[2m[32m"{{ vars.ticket }}"[0m

A column that renders empty for every row is dropped from the table. Templates, 
widths, and drop priority: custom columns config.

[1m[32mStatus symbols[0m

The Status column packs several subcolumns, left to right, each mapping to a 
schema-2 field in [2m--format=json[0m (schema 1 spells several of them differently — 
see Schema 1). Working-tree flags are independent and co-occur — any combination
 shows at once. The other subcolumns are mutually exclusive: each shows a single
 symbol, the highest-priority state in top-to-bottom table order, and is blank 
when nothing applies.

[32mWorking tree[0m

Independent flags from [2mgit status[0m; several can show at once (e.g. [2m+!?[0m). Each 
maps to a boolean in the changes object:

 Symbol worktree.changes          Meaning          
 ────── ──────────────── ───────────────────────── 
 [36m+[0m      [2mstaged[0m           Staged files              
 [36m![0m      [2mmodified[0m         Modified files (unstaged) 
 [36m?[0m      [2muntracked[0m        Untracked files           

[2mworktree.changes[0m also reports [2mrenamed[0m and [2mdeleted[0m, which have no dedicated 
symbol in the column.

[32mWorktree[0m

An in-progress git operation, a worktree-location attribute, or a branch with no
 worktree. One symbol shows, highest priority first ([2m✘ > ↻ > ⊟ > ⊞ > ⚑ > /[0m):

 Symb            JSON                               Meaning                     
  ol                                                                            
 ──── ────────────────────────── ────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 [31m✘[0m    [2mworktree.changes.conflicte[0m Merge conflicts                                
      [2md[0m                                                                         
 [33m↻[0m    [2mworktree.operation[0m         A git operation is in progress; [2mgit status[0m     
      [2m"rebase"[0m, [2m"merge"[0m,         names it                                       
      [2m"cherry_pick"[0m, [2m"revert"[0m,                                                  
      [2m"bisect"[0m                                                                  
 [33m⊟[0m    [2mworktree.prunable[0m          Prunable (worktree directory missing)          
 [33m⊞[0m    [2mworktree.locked[0m            Locked worktree                                
 [2m[33m⚑[0m    [2mworktree.duplicate_branch[0m  Branch checked out in more than one worktree,  
                                 so [2mwt[0m resolves it to whichever git lists       
                                 first; every worktree on the branch is flagged 
 [2m[33m⚑[0m    [2mworktree.branch_mismatch[0m   Worktree isn't at the path its branch implies  
                                 — including a detached one, which has no       
                                 branch to imply a path and so is never at home 
 [2m/[0m    no [2mworktree[0m object         Branch without a worktree                      

[32mDefault branch[0m

The single highest-priority state describing the branch's relation to the 
default branch; blank when none applies (a normal up-to-date branch). Each 
symbol is one [2mdisplay.state[0m value:

 Symb display.sta                            Meaning                            
  ol      te                                                                    
 ──── ─────────── ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 [2m^[0m    [2m"is_main"[0m   The main worktree (the repo's home worktree)                  
 [2m∅[0m    [2m"orphan"[0m    No common ancestor with the default branch                    
 [2m_[0m    [2m"empty"[0m     Same commit as the default branch, working tree clean — safe  
                  to remove; row dimmed                                         
 [2m⊂[0m    [2m"integrated[0m Content integrated into the default branch or merge target    
      [2m"[0m           via different history; the matching check is in               
                  [2mdefault_branch.integration.reason[0m; row dimmed                 
 [33m✗[0m    [2m"would_conf[0m Merging into the default branch would conflict (simulated     
      [2mlict"[0m       with [2mgit merge-tree[0m) and the branch isn't already integrated; 
                  with [2m--full[0m, the check includes tracked uncommitted changes   
 [2m–[0m    [2m"same_commi[0m Same commit as the default branch, but with uncommitted       
      [2mt"[0m          changes                                                       
 [2m↕[0m    [2m"diverged"[0m  Both ahead of and behind the default branch                   
 [2m↑[0m    [2m"ahead"[0m     Has commits the default branch doesn't                        
 [2m↓[0m    [2m"behind"[0m    Missing commits the default branch has                        

Rows are dimmed when safe to delete — [2m_[0m ([2m"empty"[0m) or [2m⊂[0m ([2m"integrated"[0m).

[32mRemote[0m

Relation to the tracking branch, derived from the [2mupstream.ahead[0m / 
[2mupstream.behind[0m counts; blank when there is no upstream:

 Symbol       upstream              Meaning        
 ────── ───────────────────── ──────────────────── 
 [2m|[0m      [2mahead[0m 0, [2mbehind[0m 0     In sync with remote  
 [2m⇡[0m      [2mahead[0m > 0             Ahead of remote      
 [2m⇣[0m      [2mbehind[0m > 0            Behind remote        
 [2m⇅[0m      [2mahead[0m > 0, [2mbehind[0m > 0 Diverged from remote 

[32mPlaceholder symbols[0m

These appear across all columns while the table is loading:

 Symbol                           Meaning                           
 ────── ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 [2m·[0m      Data is loading, or collection timed out / branch too stale 

[2m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[0m

[1m[32mJSON output[0m

[2m--format=json[0m emits structured data in one of two schemas while the format
migrates: [2m[list] json-schema = 2[0m selects the envelope format below, [2m= 1[0m
the original bare-array format. Unset emits schema 1 with a warning
([2mwt config update[0m adopts [2m= 2[0m); a future release flips the default to
schema 2 and later removes schema 1.

[32mSchema 2[0m

One envelope object. Items carry independent facts; rendered strings
(including the collapsed Status value) live under [2mdisplay[0m:

